      "math_academy_id": "q-274608",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine",
      "problem": "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(2)^{2}+(5)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =29 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
