      "problem": "Given that the expression $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the **exact** value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ \n{{field-1}} $)$",
      "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
