    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-327982",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine",
      "problem": "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude–phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.381\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFor the amplitude,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={2}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =29 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{29}sin\\,(x-0.381).\n$$",
